dict(w="子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 曰：「/said | ^文/(King) Wen | 武/(and King) Wu | 之/'s | 道，=dào/Way | 未/has not | 墜=zhuì/fallen | 於/to | 地，/the ground | 在/(it) is among | 人。/men | ^賢/worthy | 者/those who are | 識=zhì/remember | 其/its | 大/great | 者，/(parts) | ^不/not | 賢/worthy | 者/those who are | 識=zhì/remember | 其/its | 小/small | 者，/(parts) | ^莫/(there is) no one (who) | 不/does not | 有/have | 文/(King) Wen | 武/(and King) Wu | 之/'s | 道=dào/Way | 焉。=yān/in him | ^夫子=fū zǐ/the Master | 焉=yān/where | 不/did not | 學？/learn | ^而/and | 亦/besides | 何/what | 常=cháng/constant | 師/teacher | 之/(marks the object put first) | 有？」/did (he) have",
  l="Zigong said:\n“The Way of Wen and Wu has not fallen to the ground; it is among men.\nThose who are worthy remember its great parts;\nthose not worthy remember its small parts;\nthere is no one who does not have the Way of Wen and Wu in him.\nWhere did the Master not learn?\nAnd besides, what constant teacher did he have?”",
  i="Zigong said: “The Way of King Wen and King Wu has not dropped into the dust; it lives on in people. The worthy keep hold of the large things in it, the less worthy of the small, and there is no one without some share of it. Where was there that our Master did not learn? And why should he have kept to one teacher?”",
  n="文 and 武 are King Wen and King Wu, the founders of the Zhou (9.5, 8.20), each named by his title alone. Zhu Xi: the Way of Wen and Wu means their counsels and instructions and their achievements, together with all the rites, music and ordered forms of the Zhou (文武之道謂文王武王之謨訓功烈與凡周之禮樂文章皆是也). 墜 zhuì 'to fall'. 在人 'it is in men': Zhu Xi — there are men who can keep it in memory (在人言人有能記之者). 識 is zhì 'to remember, to keep in mind', both times (Zhu Xi: 識音志…識記也). 其大者 'its great ones', its larger parts: 者 makes 大 and 小 into things. 莫不有…焉 'there is no one who does not have … in him': 焉 is the fused 於之. The 焉 of 夫子焉不學 is yān 'where?' (Zhu Xi: 下焉字於䖍反, marking this second one): 'where did the Master not learn?' — nowhere. 何常師之有 'what constant teacher did he have?': the object 何常師 is put before the verb and marked by 之. Kong Anguo in He Yan: worthy and unworthy each had something they remembered, and there was no one the Master did not learn from (夫子無所不從學也), so he had no constant teacher (無所不從學故無常師也). Compare the Master's own words at 7.21: among three men walking there is sure to be a teacher for him."),
